In ARC stream tasks, a demo request and a wrong submission both have an explicit cost, while an instance that ends unsolved can incur the full attempt budget. Avoid reflexively requesting examples: first record the unresolved ambiguity and at least two candidate rules, then spell out how their predicted test outputs differ. Ask for another demo only if it can discriminate those alternatives and change the decision. After each response, update the hypothesis from changed and unchanged cells; do not repeat a request without a new discriminating question. If the evidence does not uniquely settle the rule, weigh the expected value of another demo against its fixed cost and the risk of a wrong submission. Keep the user-facing ARC protocol exact: emit one action JSON only.

A practical safeguard: maintain a compact change ledger (coordinate, old value, new value, component/shape, nearby unchanged counterexamples) before generalizing from examples. In particular, a single transformed object among visually similar objects means the untouched near-match is evidence that the rule is selective; explicitly characterize that distinction before applying the rule to a fresh grid.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/HA-hermes-luna6-medium-ws0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u095/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzles/continual-arc-grid-reasoning/references/demo-request-economics.md"}

